{"context":"- The CLI is the interface to the standard; vocabulary is owned by the standard identity (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §3 A7).\n- Anvil v2 adopts the software delivery standard identity; the v1.x command surface still speaks deployment-tool vocabulary (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §1.1, §2.2).\n- Lifecycle vocabulary — the terms of the domain — is owned by Anvil and must be consistent everywhere the lifecycle is described (ANVIL_MANIFESTO §4.1).\n- Two execution debts exist in the CLI: D11 (fictional exit-code contract) and D12 (ghost CLI surfaces: the legacy `runtime` group, undocumented subcommands) — plus D10's phantom `target-id` argument (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §3.2). Source: ADR-032-cli-and-vocabulary-alignment.md Date:2026-08-03 Status:Proposed.","decision":"- The command surface moves to the standard vocabulary: `adapter` → `standard`, with aliases during the deprecation window.\n- **Config key rename policy** (delegated to this ADR by ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §12.1/§12.2): adopt `project.standard`; the legacy `project.adapter` key is read as an alias with a deprecation warning and is removed at the end of the deprecation window — an explicit, governed breaking change, not an accidental one.\n- **Exit-code truthfulness (D11):** the exit-code contract is made truthful as a governed breaking fix; v2.0 is the correct moment, and automation relying on fictional codes migrates during the window.\n- **Ghost/legacy surfaces (D12):** the legacy `runtime` group and undocumented subcommands are removed per an announced deprecation schedule with warnings; ","consequences":"- **Positive:** truthful exit codes; no ghost surfaces; one vocabulary across the CLI, configuration, and documentation.\n- **Negative:** governed breaking changes at A7 completion (Phase 6): command renames, the config key rename, and exit-code truthfulness (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §12.1).\n- **Negative:** CI workflows invoking v1.x commands must migrate during the deprecation window (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §11.5); docs and the command-implementation checklist are revised (EPIC-019; §7 documentation migration).","alternativesConsidered":"Considered\n\n- **Keep the v1.x vocabulary.** Rejected: it contradicts the v2 identity; the CLI is the interface to the standard (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §3 A7).\n- **Rename silently without a deprecation window.** Rejected: v1.x users and CI workflows would break (ANVIL_V2_TRANSITION_PLAN §11.5); deprecation is a governed event, never silent (§12.5).\n- **Keep both vocabularies indefinitely.** Rejected: ghost surfaces persist (D12) and vocabulary must be consistent everywhere (ANVIL_MANIFESTO §4.1"}